        <div id="where-leaks-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where Chimney Leaks Actually Start
          </h2>

          <p>
            Chimney leaks show up as stains on the ceiling or wall beside the chimney, water in the firebox, rust on the damper, or white efflorescence on interior brick. The source is usually one of five places: the crown, the flashing, the cap, the brick and mortar, or condensation inside the flue. Several can be at fault at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The crown</strong> sits on top of the chimney and is supposed to shed water away from the brick. Many crowns are built from ordinary mortar rather than properly formed concrete, with no overhang or drip edge. They crack with temperature swings, and each crack funnels water straight into the chimney structure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Flashing</strong> seals the joint where the chimney passes through the roof. Step flashing and counterflashing should overlap and be set into the mortar joints. When sealant dries out or the counterflashing pulls away from the brick, water runs down the chimney and into the attic, often showing up some distance from the actual entry point.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The cap</strong> covers the top of the flue opening. Without one, rain falls straight down the flue, and animals and debris get in. <strong>The brick and mortar</strong> absorb rain on all four sides of the chimney; soft brick and eroded joints can carry surprising amounts of water inward. A water repellent addresses this last source and helps protect the brick from freeze-thaw damage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Diagnosing Your Chimney Before You Buy Anything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a look from the ground using binoculars. Check for cracks or missing pieces on the crown, a missing or damaged cap, flashing that looks bent or loose, dark wet patches on the brick, white staining, and spalled brick faces. Then look in the attic during or after a rain to see where water appears.
          </p>

          <p>
            A controlled hose test pinpoints leaks. With a helper inside watching for water, run a garden hose gently over one part of the chimney at a time for 10 to 15 minutes, starting low at the flashing and working upward to the brick, then the crown, then the cap. When water appears inside, you have found the source. Do one area at a time, and stop if you see water inside so you do not soak the ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a hose test is not possible, a professional chimney inspection is the reliable alternative. The National Fire Protection Association standard NFPA 211 calls for chimneys, fireplaces and vents to be inspected annually, and a sweep certified by the Chimney Safety Institute of America can do a Level 1 or Level 2 inspection, including a camera scan of the flue when needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            An inspection also catches problems a homeowner should not try to seal around, such as a cracked flue liner, deteriorated mortar inside the flue, or creosote buildup. These are fire and carbon monoxide hazards, and no exterior sealer addresses them. Fix those first, then deal with water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="breathable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why a Chimney Sealer Must Breathe
          </h2>

          <p>
            A chimney is constantly moving moisture. Rain soaks in from outside, and combustion and condensation add water from inside, especially with modern high-efficiency appliances or a fireplace used intermittently in cold weather. The brick has to release that moisture through its outer face, or it builds up in the masonry.
          </p>

          <p>
            When water trapped in brick freezes, it expands and fractures the surface. Repeated winters of this produce spalling: chunks of brick face popping off, often visible as debris on the roof or in the gutter. Mortar joints erode similarly. Non-breathable coatings, including many paints, gloss acrylics and roofing tars, make this worse by sealing water in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breathable repellents, siloxane and silane-siloxane formulas in particular, line the pores of brick and mortar with a water-repellent layer without filling them. Rain beads and runs off, but vapor still escapes. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> covers the same breathable chemistries for flatwork. Chimney professionals and repellent makers stress vapor permeability on their data sheets for exactly this reason. For the chemistry behind the two families, see <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane sealers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same principle applies to the house walls if they are brick. Our <a href="/best-brick-sealer-reviews/">brick sealer guide</a> covers products for walls, patios and garden structures, many of which overlap with chimney-suitable repellents.
          </p>

