        <div id="crown" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Dealing With the Crown
          </h2>

          <p>
            Inspect the crown up close if you can do so safely. Hairline cracks on an otherwise solid crown are the ideal case for an elastomeric crown coating like ChimneyRX or CrownSeal. Clean off moss, debris and loose material, fill wider cracks with the patching compound the coating maker recommends, let everything dry, and brush on the coating in the thickness the label specifies.
          </p>

          <p>
            A crown that is crumbling, broken into separate pieces, or built as a thin mortar wash with no overhang needs to be rebuilt. A proper crown is cast concrete, sloped to shed water, with an overhang of a couple of inches past the brick and a drip edge underneath so water falls clear rather than running down the face. A bond break around the flue liner lets the liner expand separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crown rebuilds are a job for a mason or chimney contractor, typically costing several hundred to well over a thousand dollars depending on chimney size, roof pitch and access. That is significantly more than a coating, but a properly built crown can last decades, while patching a failed one buys only a season or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep crown coatings off the flue liner unless the product instructions specifically permit it, and never coat the inside of the flue. The flue must vent combustion gases, and coatings are not rated for those temperatures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flashing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Flashing and Caps: The Other Half of the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flashing failures are the most common source of leaks that appear around the chimney inside the house. Look for counterflashing that has pulled out of the mortar joints, dried and cracked sealant along the top edge, rusted or holed metal, and missing step flashing pieces. Small gaps in counterflashing can sometimes be resealed with a high-quality polyurethane or flashing-rated sealant.
          </p>

          <p>
            More serious flashing damage needs replacement, which usually means removing shingles around the chimney, installing new step flashing and counterflashing cut into the mortar joints, and reshingling. That is roofing work, and it should be done by a roofer or chimney contractor. Sealant smeared over badly failed flashing is a temporary fix at best.
          </p>

          <p>
            A chimney cap keeps rain, snow, birds and animals out of the flue. Caps are inexpensive relative to the damage they prevent, and many come in stainless steel with a mesh screen that also acts as a spark arrestor. Measure the flue tile, or have the sweep fit one during an inspection.
          </p>

          <p>
            For caulking choices around flashing and other exterior joints, see our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">exterior caulk guide</a>. Choose sealants rated for metal-to-masonry joints and for the temperature range the chimney sees, and avoid silicone where paint or another sealant may need to bond later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Apply Chimney Water Repellent
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work on a cool, dry chimney. The fireplace and any appliances venting through the chimney should be off and cold for at least a day. The masonry should be dry, with no rain in the previous 24 hours or more, and the forecast should be clear for the cure window on the label. Mild temperatures, roughly 40 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit, suit most products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the brick first if it is dirty, mossy or stained. Use a masonry cleaner and a stiff brush; avoid high-pressure washing on chimney brick, which erodes mortar. Repair spalled bricks and eroded mortar joints, and let new mortar cure for the time its maker specifies before applying repellent. Fix the crown and flashing at the same time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the roof, windows and siding with plastic sheeting or drop cloths. Using a low-pressure pump sprayer, apply repellent to the brick from the top of the chimney down, flooding the surface so it runs a few inches below the spray. Apply a second coat wet on wet if the label calls for it. Cover all four sides, including the side facing away from you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the repellent cure for the time on the label before rain. Results are not fully apparent for a few days; after that, water should bead on the brick. Recheck the chimney after the first heavy rain to confirm the leak has stopped, and if it has not, revisit the crown and flashing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Roof work is the dangerous part</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use a ladder rated for your weight plus tools, tie it off, and set it at a safe angle. On steep or high roofs, use a fall-arrest harness and roof anchor, or hire the job out. Never spray repellent into the flue or onto a warm chimney, and keep solvent products well away from any open flame or pilot light.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Chimney Waterproofing Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Materials for a do-it-yourself repellent job are modest. A typical single-flue chimney might have 100 to 200 square feet of brick above the roofline, which a gallon or two of repellent will cover depending on porosity. At the time of writing, that means roughly $40 to $200 in repellent, plus a pump sprayer and plastic sheeting.
          </p>

          <p>
            A crown coating kit runs roughly $40 to $90. Flashing sealant is a few dollars a tube. A stainless steel chimney cap usually costs $50 to $200 for the part, more for multi-flue or custom sizes. All in, a DIY package of repellent, crown coating, flashing touch-ups and a cap is often under $500.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional chimney waterproofing, often bundled with an inspection and crown coating, typically ranges from a few hundred dollars to around $1,000. Crown rebuilds, flashing replacement and repointing add substantially, and major masonry repair on a deteriorated chimney can reach several thousand dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weigh that against the cost of ignoring a wet chimney. Water damage to framing, drywall and insulation around a chimney is expensive to repair, and chimneys that spall badly may need partial rebuilding above the roofline. Annual inspections and a repellent every several years are cheap by comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Inspection and Maintenance Schedule
          </h2>

          <p>
            Have the chimney inspected once a year, as NFPA 211 recommends, ideally before the heating season. The sweep checks the flue, liner, damper and firebox for safety problems and can spot early crown cracks, flashing gaps and mortar erosion before they cause leaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the repellent every year or two with a simple hose test on a sample area. When the brick darkens and stays dark rather than beading water, it is time to reapply. Penetrating repellents do not need stripping; clean the brick and spray a fresh coat. Many manufacturers claim service lives of 5 to 10 years or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the crown coating annually. Elastomeric coatings can wear, especially on south-facing exposures with strong sun. Touch up any thin spots or new cracks before winter. Check the cap for rust or damage and confirm it is secure after windstorms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for warning signs between inspections: brick fragments on the roof or in the gutter, white staining on the chimney, rust on the damper, a musty smell from the fireplace in wet weather, or water stains on nearby ceilings. Each points to a specific failure that is cheaper to fix early.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spalling-repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Repointing and Replacing Damaged Chimney Brick
          </h2>

          <p>
            Repellent protects sound masonry; it does not restore damaged masonry. Before treating a chimney, walk through every face and note bricks with broken faces, joints recessed more than about a quarter inch, and mortar that crumbles when scraped with a screwdriver. Those spots let water in faster than any sealer can keep it out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repointing means grinding or raking out deteriorated mortar to a depth of roughly two to three times the joint width and packing in fresh mortar in layers. The new mortar should be compatible with the old, which on older chimneys often means a softer, lime-rich mix rather than a hard modern one. Mortar harder than the brick forces movement and moisture into the brick, which then spalls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Individual spalled bricks can be cut out and replaced with matching salvaged or new brick. Where many bricks above the roofline have failed, a partial rebuild from the roofline up is often the most cost-effective repair, and it is a good moment to add a proper crown and new flashing.
          </p>

          <p>
            New mortar needs to cure before repellent goes on, and the wait varies by mix and weather; mortar makers commonly suggest several weeks. Plan masonry repairs for late spring or summer and the repellent for early fall, so the chimney goes into winter repaired, cured and protected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inside-the-flue" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. What Not to Seal: The Flue, Firebox and Liner
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product on this list is for the outside of the chimney. None is rated for the inside of a flue, the firebox, or the smoke chamber, which see high temperatures, corrosive combustion byproducts and creosote. Spraying a repellent or coating there can create flammable residue or release fumes when the fireplace is next used.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flue liners that are cracked, missing tiles, or have open mortar joints need repair with methods designed for them, such as a stainless steel relining kit or a heat-rated cast-in-place or resurfacing system installed by a professional. A damaged liner allows heat and gases to reach combustible framing and can let carbon monoxide escape into living spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Interior firebox brick with cracked or missing mortar joints is repaired with refractory mortar rated for fireplace temperatures, not with masonry sealer or standard mortar. Firebrick that is cracked through should be replaced.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you notice smoke in the room, a strong creosote smell, or soot staining on the chimney exterior, stop using the fireplace and have the system inspected before any waterproofing work. Water problems can wait a week; a compromised flue cannot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using paint, tar or a gloss sealer on chimney brick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Non-breathable coatings trap moisture inside chimney masonry, where it freezes and breaks off the brick face. Roofing tar smeared on brick or crowns also cracks and holds water. Use only repellents described as vapor permeable on the brick, and reserve coatings for the crown with products designed for that surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing brick while the real leak is the flashing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many leaks blamed on brick come from failed counterflashing or a cracked crown. Sealing the brick alone leaves those paths open, and the leak continues. Hose-test the chimney section by section to find the source, then fix the crown and flashing along with sealing the brick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a warm or recently used chimney</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Applying repellent to warm masonry can cause it to flash off before it penetrates, and solvent products near heat are a fire hazard. Let the fireplace and any appliance venting through the chimney sit unused and cold for at least a day before working, and schedule the job for a mild, overcast morning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping repairs to spalled brick and mortar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer cannot rebuild a broken brick face or fill eroded joints. Spalled bricks and crumbling mortar let water deep into the chimney. Replace damaged brick, repoint the joints, let the mortar cure as directed, then apply repellent. Otherwise the sealer is wasted on surfaces that still leak.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking risks on the roof</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most chimney work happens at height on a sloped surface. Unsecured ladders, wet shingles and working alone cause serious falls. Use a properly secured ladder, a roof harness on steep pitches, and a helper on the ground. If the roof is high or steep, hire a chimney professional.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a regular masonry repellent fine for a chimney?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes, as long as it is breathable. Many silane-siloxane repellents sold for brick walls are suitable for chimney brick. Check that the label describes the product as vapor permeable and rates it for above-grade vertical masonry. Avoid film-forming sealers and paints. Chimney-specific products like ChimneySaver are formulated with especially high vapor permeability for chimney conditions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are bricks on my chimney crumbling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most often from freeze-thaw damage. Water soaks into the brick, freezes, expands and breaks off the face, a process called spalling. Soft brick, eroded mortar, a failed crown or a non-breathable coating all make it worse. Replace damaged bricks, repoint mortar, fix the crown, and apply a breathable repellent to slow future damage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealing the brick stop my chimney leak?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the brick is the source. Many chimney leaks come from cracked crowns, failed flashing or a missing cap. A hose test, done one area at a time with a helper inside, shows where water enters. Sealing the brick is still worthwhile to protect the masonry, but it should be combined with repairs to whichever part is actually leaking.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does chimney water repellent last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Quality penetrating repellents are commonly rated for 5 to 10 years or more by their makers, depending on exposure and brick porosity. South- and west-facing sides and heavily exposed chimneys wear faster. Check the brick with a hose every year or two; when it darkens and stays dark instead of beading water, reapply.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to coat the chimney crown separately?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Crowns are horizontal, take the most water, and crack from temperature swings. A standard brick repellent does not bridge cracks. Use an elastomeric crown coating on a sound crown with hairline cracks, and rebuild crowns that are crumbling or lack an overhang. Keep coatings off the flue liner unless the product label allows it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a homeowner waterproof a chimney?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many can, if the roof is low-pitched and easily reached with a secured ladder. The work itself, cleaning, spraying and brushing on a crown coating, is straightforward. Steep or high roofs, multi-story chimneys, and any flashing or crown rebuilds are better left to a chimney or roofing professional with proper fall protection and equipment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a repellent make the chimney look wet or shiny?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating repellents are designed to dry clear, with no sheen or change in color. Brick may look darker for a few hours while wet with product, then return to normal. Crown coatings are visible but sit on top of the chimney where few people see them. Avoid gloss sealers, which change the look and trap moisture.
              </p>
